7. U10's real circuit — route C (discrete), decided on resistance, not preference
Condition 2 of the Rev B review (MS-CAR-REV-B-001 §6.4) asks for reverse-polarity protection, an over-voltage cut-off that opens before the rail reaches 5.5 V, survival of 12 V or 24 V on J18 indefinitely, and a current limit near 3.5 A. The review names TI's TPS26630 / TPS26631 with an external blocking FET.

Part data checked against TI's own documentation (accessed 1 Nov 2026):
TPS2663x datasheet Rev. G, ti.com/lit/ds/symlink/tps2663.pdf — 4.5-60 V operating, 67 V abs max, 31 mΩ, 0.6-6 A adjustable limit, adjustable OVP cut-off, B-FET driver for reverse polarity. Packages: VQFN (RGE) 24 pins for TPS26630; HTSSOP (PWP) 20 pins and VQFN for TPS26631. Not SOT-23-6.
TPS2662x datasheet Rev. F, ti.com/lit/ds/symlink/tps2662.pdf — 4.5-60 V but only 0.88 A, 10-lead 3 x 3 VSON.

So the SOT-23-6 placeholder cannot host it: 6 pins cannot give a 3.5 A limit behind a 60 V front end. U10 has to change footprint before it can have a circuit — a layout change in the input bay, not a wiring job.

Decision (owner, 1 Nov): route C — a discrete OVP / reverse-polarity controller driving a logic-level N-FET. The arithmetic, not the part list, decides it. Sheet resistance 0.505 mΩ/sq at 1 oz, 1.01 mΩ/sq at 0.5 oz on the inner layers (note: the board file carries no stackup, so these weights are the owner's, not read out of the project):

Allocation	Route C	TPS2663x
J18 terminal + F1 clip contacts	10 mΩ	10 mΩ
Protection switch	5 mΩ (logic-level N-FET)	31 mΩ (internal FET, typ)
Input pour, 4 mm × ~120 mm	15 mΩ	15 mΩ
Vias and misc	10 mΩ	10 mΩ
Total	40 mΩ — 20 mΩ margin	66 mΩ — over before any track

A and B cannot hold condition 5's 60 mΩ on the switch alone. VQFN-24 was rejected separately on hand assembly (F2) and the open via-in-pad question (R16); deferring was rejected because R4 is P0 and condition 2 is one of the six accepted on the record.

What route C still needs before it can be drawn, and is not yet verified: the controller must either have a charge pump or drive a FET that enhances fully at 5 V, because this bus is 5.15 V nominal and most 60 V controllers want 8-10 V of gate drive. That is to be checked against datasheets. If nothing satisfies both 5 V gate drive and a 5.4 V trip point, the fallback is TPS26631 in HTSSOP-20, with a written request to Serigreen to relax 60 mΩ to 90 mΩ rather than miss it silently.

Governance: the review names TPS26630, so route C is a deviation from a part the reviewer specified. It must go to Serigreen as an Agreed item on the response sheet before it is drawn — not as a silent substitution. The table above is the one-line question to send them; drawing proceeds in parallel so the answer is not on the critical path.

Resistance note that changes the J5 row: the modem feed as it stood was 17.08 mm of 0.3 mm on In2.Cu — 57 squares at 1.01 mΩ = ~58 mΩ, the whole 60 mΩ budget on its own. Widened in place tonight to 1.5 mm it is 11.4 squares × 1.01 = 11.5 mΩ. The same run on F.Cu at 1.5 mm would be 5.8 mΩ, and as a 4 mm pour on F.Cu 2.2 mΩ. In-place widening was the instruction and it is legal, but it leaves ~5.7 mΩ on the inner layer against an outer-layer re-route — worth having once route C's 20 mΩ margin is being spent.

